<%# One game on the league scoreboard. Locals: game (Game).

    THE ROW IS A TEAM CARD, and it wears the app's EXISTING team-card
    convention rather than a second one invented here: `team_card_palette`
    (TeamColorsHelper), the same helper behind contests/_multi_week_team_card,
    contests/_turf_totals_board and teams/index.

    Using it is not just tidiness. The naive reading of "dark field, light ink"
    is `card_background` + `card_mascot`, and measured across the league that
    pair falls below WCAG AA for 14 of 32 teams and below 3:1 for 11 — Buffalo
    is red-on-blue at 1.88:1, Tampa Bay red-on-grey at 2.03:1. The helper
    already solves it: the FIELD stays the team's own hue, while `fg` resolves
    to white on a dark field and near-black on a light one, so every team is
    legible and no team stops looking like itself. The mascot colour keeps its
    job as the ACCENT — the rail, the scoring wash, the touchdown sweep.

    The name line is the MASCOT ("Giants", "Ravens"), not the ESPN
    abbreviation — that is a feed detail, and the location line already says
    "New York".

    Animation hooks:
      · data-game-slug / data-team-slug — how the page finds the row that scored
      · --nfl-team / --nfl-team-ink     — field hue and accent, read by every animation
      · .nfl-row-wash                   — the flash overlay
      · .nfl-sweep                      — the touchdown's left-to-right strip %>
<% home = game.home_team %>
<% away = game.away_team %>
<%# CONTEST EXTRAS. Both default to the league board's own values, so /live
    renders byte-for-byte what it did before the contest live page adopted this
    tile — which is the point of sharing the partial rather than copying it.

      scorers  true draws the chronological scoring-play strip.
      size     :hero enlarges the type for the contest page, where this tile is
               the full-width centrepiece rather than one of four across. Only
               type and spacing change; the structure, the colours and every
               animation hook are identical, so nothing downstream has to know
               which size it is looking at. %>
<% scorers = local_assigns.fetch(:scorers, false) %>
<% hero    = local_assigns.fetch(:size, :default).to_sym == :hero %>
<%# THE CARD HOSTS THE GLOW RING, and the clipping moved inside it.
    `.studio-team-glow` paints ::before/::after at a NEGATIVE inset, so the ring
    lives OUTSIDE the host's box — and `overflow-hidden` on the host would clip
    away the entire effect. The rows still need clipping (their gradients have
    to stop at the rounded corners), so that job moved to an inner box which
    inherits the card's radius.
    The engine's own note on this class says it punches the host's box out of
    the wedge layers precisely so a LIVE BOARD CARD can wear the ring directly,
    without a wrapper Turbo would orphan on replace. This is that case.
    Opacity starts at 0 — the ring is dark until a score lights it, and the
    class's built-in 0.4s opacity transition gives the fade for free. %>
<div class="card p-0 transition-shadow duration-300 studio-team-glow"
     data-game-slug="<%= game.slug %>"
     data-test="live-game-tile"
     style="--studio-team-glow-opacity: 0;
            --studio-team-glow-thickness: 3px;
            --studio-team-glow-bloom: 12px;
            --studio-team-glow-speed: 5s">
  <%# The hero card is given a floor and its two rows share it. Without one the
      card is only as tall as two lines of text, and the events rail — the thing
      that is supposed to EVOLVE — showed three plays and clipped the fourth. %>
  <div class="relative <%= "flex flex-col min-h-[15rem]" if hero %>" style="overflow: hidden; border-radius: inherit">
    <% [[away, game.away_score], [home, game.home_score]].each_with_index do |(team, score), index| %>
      <% palette = team_card_palette(team) %>
      <% field = normalize_hex(team&.card_background) || "#3a3f4b" %>
      <div class="relative flex items-center overflow-hidden <%= hero ? "flex-1 gap-4 pl-5 pr-[35%] py-4" : "gap-3 px-3 py-2.5" %>"
           data-team-slug="<%= team&.slug %>"
           data-role="team-row"
           style="background: <%= palette[:gradient] %>;
                  --nfl-team: <%= field %>;
                  --nfl-team-ink: <%= palette[:accent] %>;
                  <%= "border-bottom: 1px solid #{palette[:divider]};" if index.zero? %>">
        <%# Flash overlay and touchdown sweep, both in the ACCENT colour and both
            parked at opacity 0 until an animation class runs them. %>
        <span class="nfl-row-wash absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none opacity-0"
              style="background: <%= palette[:accent] %>"></span>
        <%# WIDTH IS INLINE, NOT A UTILITY CLASS, and that is load-bearing. The
            sweep travels by translateX in PERCENT — percentages of its own box —
            so a width that fails to apply does not make the strip narrow, it
            makes it travel exactly zero pixels while still fading in and out,
            which reads as a stationary glow with no error anywhere.
            It was a STALE BUILD that produced that here — the utility was
            written after the last tailwind compile, not purged (the class is in
            the build now and this file is scanned). Inline anyway: a dimension
            an animation's arithmetic depends on should not be able to go missing
            because of build ordering. %>
        <span class="nfl-sweep absolute inset-y-0 left-0 pointer-events-none opacity-0"
              style="width: 34%;
                     background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, <%= palette[:accent] %>, transparent)"></span>

        <span class="nfl-rail relative rounded-full flex-shrink-0 <%= hero ? "w-1.5 h-14" : "w-1 h-8" %>"
              style="background: <%= palette[:accent] %>"></span>
        <span class="relative leading-none flex-shrink-0 <%= hero ? "text-4xl" : "text-xl" %>"
              style="text-shadow: <%= palette[:mascot_shadow] %>"><%= team&.emoji %></span>
        <%# Same colour convention as contests/_multi_week_team_card: the MASCOT
            in the team accent over its legibility halo, the CITY in the light
            location colour. The halo is what makes the accent readable on the
            team's own field — it is why Buffalo's red-on-blue (1.88:1 unaided)
            works here exactly as it does on the contests card. %>
        <span class="relative min-w-0 flex-1">
          <span class="block font-extrabold truncate <%= hero ? "text-4xl leading-tight" : "text-sm" %>"
                style="color: <%= palette[:accent] %>; text-shadow: <%= palette[:mascot_shadow] %>">
            <%= team&.mascot.presence || team&.short_name || team&.name %>
          </span>
          <span class="block font-medium truncate <%= hero ? "text-lg" : "text-[11px]" %>" style="color: <%= palette[:location] %>">
            <%= team&.location %>
          </span>
        </span>
        <%# NO inline colour on the score, unlike the name and location above.
            An inline style beats a stylesheet rule, so the hot state could never
            repaint it — the score reads its resting colour from --nfl-team-fg
            through a rule instead, which the hot rule can then override. %>
        <span class="relative font-extrabold tabular-nums <%= hero ? "text-5xl" : "text-2xl" %>"
              style="--nfl-team-fg: <%= palette[:fg] %>"
              data-role="score"><%= score.to_i %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# THE RIGHT QUARTER: when the game happens, where it is up to, and what has
        happened so far. Hero only — the league board's tiles are 300px wide and
        have nowhere to put it.

        IT DOES NOT CUT THE CARD IN TWO. The team rows still run the full width,
        gradients and all, and this sits ON them behind a scrim that fades in
        from transparent — so the colour carries all the way across and the two
        areas are separated by legibility rather than by a line. The rows simply
        stop their CONTENT at the 73% mark, which is why the scores land where
        they do with nothing drawn between. %>
    <% if hero %>
      <div class="absolute inset-y-0 right-0 w-1/3 flex flex-col items-center text-center px-4 pt-7 pb-5 pointer-events-none"
           data-test="live-focus-rail"
           style="background: linear-gradient(90deg,
                  rgba(8, 10, 16, 0) 0%, rgba(8, 10, 16, 0.55) 24%, rgba(8, 10, 16, 0.8) 55%)">
        <%# Date and kickoff are BOTH written by the browser in the reader's own
            zone — the app runs on UTC, so a server-rendered date is a day out
            for anyone west of it. The text inside each is a truthful fallback. %>
        <%# A FIXED BOX, and the height is the whole point. These three lines are
            optional in different combinations — a scheduled game has no quarter,
            a game with no kickoff has neither date nor time — so left to size
            themselves they made the block a different height per game, and
            clicking through the strip jogged the date, the clock and the top of
            the events feed up and down. Reserving the space once means switching
            games changes the CONTENT and nothing else. Its own contents stay
            centred inside it, so a scheduled game does not read as top-heavy. %>
        <div class="h-[4.75rem] flex-shrink-0 flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-1">
          <% if game.kickoff_at %>
            <time datetime="<%= game.kickoff_at.utc.iso8601 %>" data-role="kickoff-date"
                  class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-white/60"><%= game.kickoff_at.utc.strftime("%b %-d") %></time>
            <time datetime="<%= game.kickoff_at.utc.iso8601 %>" data-role="kickoff"
                  class="text-lg font-bold text-white/90 leading-none"><%= game.kickoff_at.utc.strftime("%a %-I:%M %p") %></time>
          <% end %>

          <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-2">
            <% if game.live? %>
              <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-400">
                <span class="h-2 w-2 rounded-full bg-red-500 animate-pulse"></span> Live
              </span>
            <% elsif game.completed? %>
              <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-white/70">Final</span>
            <% end %>
            <% if game.status_detail.present? && !game.status_detail.casecmp("final").zero? %>
              <span class="text-sm font-mono text-white/80 truncate"><%= game.status_detail %></span>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </div>

        <%# THE EVENTS, newest first. A feed that grows downward pushes the play
            you care about out of a fixed-height rail; newest-first keeps the last
            score where the eye already is, and the rest scroll. %>
        <% plays = game.goals.sort_by { |goal| [goal.minute || 0, goal.id] }.reverse %>
        <% if plays.any? %>
          <%# THE FADE IS AN OVERLAY, NOT A MASK ON THE SCROLLER, and that choice
              is what keeps a two-play game honest. A mask fades the container's
              last 24px whether or not anything is under them, so a short list
              would sit there half-dissolved, promising a scroll it does not
              have. These two gradients paint the rail's OWN colour over the
              feed's ends: where a play is under one it fades out and says
              "there is more"; where the list is short they cover empty space in
              the colour already there, and are invisible. %>
          <div class="relative mt-1 flex-1 min-h-0 w-full flex flex-col">
            <div class="tt-event-feed min-h-0 overflow-y-auto space-y-1 pointer-events-auto"
                 data-test="live-focus-events">
              <% plays.each do |play| %>
                <% scoring_team = play.team_slug == game.home_team_slug ? home : away %>
                <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-1.5 text-xs leading-tight whitespace-nowrap">
                  <span class="text-sm"><%= scoring_team&.emoji %></span>
                  <span class="text-white/85 font-medium truncate"><%= play.scoring_label.presence || "Score" %></span>
                  <% if play.points %>
                    <span class="font-mono font-bold text-white/60">+<%= play.points %></span>
                  <% end %>
                </div>
              <% end %>
            </div>
            <span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-5"
                  style="background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(8, 10, 16, 0.92), rgba(8, 10, 16, 0))"></span>
            <span class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 h-7"
                  style="background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(8, 10, 16, 0.92), rgba(8, 10, 16, 0))"></span>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# Status strip. `status_detail` is ESPN's own shortDetail ("Final",
      "Q3 8:42", "Halftime"), which says more than our three-state status
      column can — so it leads, and the column is only the fallback. %>
  <%# The right-hand detail is ESPN's own shortDetail, which usually says MORE
      than the badge on the left ("Q3 8:42", "Halftime", "Final/OT"). Sometimes
      it says exactly the same thing, and a concluded game then printed
      "Final          Final". Suppressed when it merely repeats the badge, kept
      whenever it adds something — so "Final/OT" still survives. %>
  <% badge = game.live? ? "Live" : (game.completed? ? "Final" : nil) %>
  <% unless hero %>
  <% detail = game.status_detail.presence %>
  <% detail = nil if detail && badge && detail.casecmp(badge).zero? %>
  <div class="bg-surface-alt/60 flex items-center justify-between gap-2 <%= hero ? "px-5 py-2.5" : "px-3 py-1.5" %>">
    <% if game.live? %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-400 <%= hero ? "text-xs" : "text-[10px]" %>">
        <span class="rounded-full bg-red-500 animate-pulse <%= hero ? "h-2 w-2" : "h-1.5 w-1.5" %>"></span> Live
      </span>
    <% elsif game.completed? %>
      <span class="font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted <%= hero ? "text-xs" : "text-[10px]" %>">Final</span>
    <% elsif game.kickoff_at %>
      <%# A MACHINE-READABLE timestamp the browser formats in the VIEWER's zone.
          The app runs on UTC (config.time_zone is commented out), so a
          server-rendered 7:00 PM Eastern kickoff printed as "Thu 11:00 PM" —
          wrong for every reader. Picking one zone server-side would only move
          the error to everyone outside it; the browser already knows the right
          answer. The text inside is a truthful no-JS fallback, labelled UTC so
          it is never silently four hours out. %>
      <time datetime="<%= game.kickoff_at.utc.iso8601 %>"
            data-role="kickoff"
            class="font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted <%= hero ? "text-xs" : "text-[10px]" %>"><%= game.kickoff_at.utc.strftime("%a %-I:%M %p") %> UTC</time>
    <% else %>
      <span class="font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted <%= hero ? "text-xs" : "text-[10px]" %>">TBD</span>
    <% end %>
    <span class="text-secondary font-mono truncate <%= hero ? "text-sm" : "text-[11px]" %>"><%= detail %></span>
  </div>

  <%# SCORING PLAYS, chronological. The score above says what the game stands at;
      this says how it got there. Scrolls sideways rather than wrapping, so a
      shoot-out cannot make one card twice the height of its neighbours in a row
      whose cards all stretch to the tallest. %>
  <% end %>

  <% if scorers && !hero %>
    <% plays = game.goals.sort_by { |goal| [goal.minute || 0, goal.id] } %>
    <% if plays.any? %>
      <div class="border-t border-subtle flex items-center overflow-x-auto <%= hero ? "px-5 py-2.5 gap-5" : "px-3 py-1.5 gap-3" %>"
           data-test="live-game-scorers">
        <% plays.each do |play| %>
          <% scoring_team = play.team_slug == game.home_team_slug ? home : away %>
          <span class="flex items-center gap-1 flex-shrink-0 leading-none whitespace-nowrap <%= hero ? "text-sm" : "text-[11px]" %>">
            <span class="<%= hero ? "text-base" : "text-[10px]" %>"><%= scoring_team&.emoji %></span>
            <span class="text-secondary font-medium">
              <%= play.player&.name&.split&.last || play.scoring_label.presence || scoring_team&.short_name || "Score" %>
            </span>
            <% if play.points %>
              <span class="text-muted font-mono tabular-nums">+<%= play.points %></span>
            <% end %>
          </span>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
